This is general information for B2B teams running outbound, not legal advice. Provider rules change, so open the linked pages before you rely on any figure here. Figures below were read from those pages on 30 October 2026.

## The short answer: what Gmail and Yahoo require

Gmail and Yahoo sender requirements come down to three things: prove who you are, keep spam complaints low, and make unsubscribing easy. Both providers apply the first two to every sender. The stricter parts, DMARC and a working one-click unsubscribe, are aimed at bulk senders.

Google's [email sender guidelines](https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126) say that since 1 February 2024 all senders emailing Gmail accounts must meet its baseline requirements. Yahoo's [sender best practices](https://senders.yahooinc.com/best-practices/) say enforcement of its standards began in February 2024, with a gradual rollout.

| | Gmail | Yahoo |
|---|---|---|
| Authentication, all senders | SPF or DKIM | SPF or DKIM at a minimum |
| Authentication, bulk senders | SPF, DKIM and DMARC | SPF and DKIM, plus a valid DMARC policy of at least p=none that passes |
| Spam rate | Keep it below 0.3% in Postmaster Tools | Keep it below 0.3% |
| Unsubscribe | One-click unsubscribe for marketing and subscribed messages from bulk senders | A functioning list-unsubscribe header for bulk senders; requests honored within 2 days |
| Bulk threshold | More than 5,000 messages per day to Gmail accounts | Not defined on the page |

## Who counts as a bulk sender

Google defines the bulk tier by volume: senders who send more than 5,000 messages per day to Gmail accounts. That is the origin of the "5,000 emails per day rule" you see quoted. Google's guidelines explain how the count works, so read that section rather than relying on a summary.

Yahoo is vaguer. Its best-practices page asks bulk senders for stronger authentication and an unsubscribe header, but it does not publish a volume figure. If you send to Yahoo addresses at any scale, plan for the bulk requirements rather than hunting for a number.

## What each provider requires

**Authentication.** SPF and DKIM are the two checks that tie a message to the domain that sent it. Google asks all senders for SPF or DKIM, and bulk senders for all three of SPF, DKIM and DMARC. Yahoo asks for the same pairing at minimum and, for bulk senders, a DMARC policy that passes. Both mention key length: Google says sending to personal Gmail accounts requires a DKIM key of 1024 bits or longer and recommends 2048 bits where your provider supports it. Our [SPF, DKIM and DMARC explainer](/resources/glossary/spf-dkim-dmarc) covers what each one does in plain English.

**Spam rate.** (Our [spam complaint rate](/resources/glossary/spam-complaint-rate) entry has a worked example.) Google says to keep the spam rate reported in Postmaster Tools below 0.3%. In its monitoring section it goes further: stay below 0.10% and avoid ever reaching 0.30% or higher. Yahoo says to keep your spam rate below 0.3%, calculated from mail delivered to the inbox.

**Unsubscribe.** Google requires marketing and subscribed messages from bulk senders to support one-click unsubscribe, using the List-Unsubscribe and List-Unsubscribe-Post headers. Yahoo asks bulk senders for a functioning list-unsubscribe header, recommends the RFC 8058 one-click method, and expects requests to be honored within 2 days.

## Do cold email teams actually hit the 5,000 threshold?

Usually not on a per-day basis. A cold outbound programme built on targeted lists tends to send far fewer than 5,000 messages a day to Gmail accounts, so the bulk tier often does not apply by volume alone.

That does not make the rules irrelevant. The baseline applies to everyone: authenticate your sending domain and keep spam complaints low. The spam rate is the requirement cold senders break first, because a recipient who never asked to hear from you is the one most likely to hit "report spam". A list that is loosely targeted can push complaints up long before volume becomes a problem. This is why list quality and a clear, honest first line matter more to deliverability than any header setting. Our [cold email deliverability guide](/resources/blogs/cold-email-deliverability-guide) covers the operating side, and the [sender reputation](/resources/glossary/sender-reputation) and [domain warmup](/resources/glossary/domain-warmup) entries explain why new domains need care.

## What the rules do not say about cold email

Neither provider's page uses the words "cold email". Google's one-click requirement is written for "marketing messages and subscribed messages", and Yahoo's for marketing and subscribed mail. Whether a one-to-one prospecting email counts as marketing is not something either page settles.

The sensible reading is cautious. Include a visible way to opt out in every message, honour it quickly, and do not build a programme that only works if the rules turn out not to apply to you. Separately, the law is a different question from provider rules. For CAN-SPAM, GDPR, India's DPDP Act and CASL, see our [cold email compliance guide](/resources/blogs/cold-email-compliance-guide).

## A pre-send checklist

Run through this before any new domain or mailbox sends its first campaign:

1. SPF or DKIM passes on the sending domain, and DKIM uses a 1024-bit key or longer (2048 where supported).
2. A DMARC record is published on the domain, even if you start at p=none.
3. Every message carries a working unsubscribe, with the List-Unsubscribe headers set where your sending tool supports it.
4. Unsubscribe requests are processed within 2 days at the most, and suppressed contacts are never re-added.
5. You can see your spam rate: set up Google Postmaster Tools for the domain and check it weekly.
6. The list is targeted. If many of the recipients could plausibly mark you as spam, fix the list before the send, not after the complaints arrive.

If you would rather not manage this yourself, our [managed cold email infrastructure](/services/cold-email) handles authentication, warmup and monitoring as part of the campaign setup.
